{
  "skillId": "hashing",
  "title": "Hashing: instant lookups",
  "minutes": 5,
  "version": 1,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "Turn O(n²) into O(n)",
      "body": "*\"Find two numbers that add up to the target.\"* The obvious answer checks every pair, which is O(n²). With a **HashMap** you remember what you’ve seen and look it up **instantly**. That trick solves a huge share of interview questions.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll watch it find a pair in one pass, and use a set to spot a repeat."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "import java.util.HashMap;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};\n        int target = 9;\n        HashMap<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>(); //~ value → index, for every number we have already passed.\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            int need = target - nums[i]; //~ The partner this number needs.\n            if (seen.containsKey(need)) { //~ Seen it already? One O(1) lookup, with no second loop.\n                System.out.println(seen.get(need) + \" \" + i);\n            }\n            seen.put(nums[i], i); //~ Remember this number for the ones after it.\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "1 0",
          "why": "The partner was seen EARLIER, so its index is printed first."
        },
        {
          "text": "1 2",
          "why": "7 + 11 is 18, not 9. The pair is 2 and 7."
        },
        {
          "text": "0 1",
          "why": "Right: at i = 1 (7), it needs 2, which was seen at index 0."
        },
        {
          "text": "2 7",
          "why": "It prints the two indexes, not the values."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
