    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Why hashing is fast",
      "points": [
        "A hash function turns a key into a bucket number, so `get`, `put` and `contains` are **O(1)** on average.",
        "`HashMap` stores key → value, and `HashSet` stores keys only.",
        "The trade: a bit more **memory** for a lot more **speed**. Pair-finding goes from O(n²) to O(n).",
        "Putting an existing key again **replaces** its value; keys are unique."
      ],
      "pattern": "Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();\nfor (int x : a) {\n    if (seen.contains(k - x)) { /* found a pair */ }\n    seen.add(x);\n}"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "import java.util.HashMap;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        HashMap<String, Integer> m = new HashMap<>();\n        m.put(\"a\", 1);\n        m.put(\"a\", 2);\n        m.put(\"b\", 3);\n        System.out.println(m.size() + \" \" + m.get(\"a\"));\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "3 2",
          "why": "The second put(\"a\", …) updates the existing key rather than adding a new one."
        },
        {
          "text": "2 1",
          "why": "The later put overwrote 1 with 2."
        },
        {
          "text": "3 1",
          "why": "A key appears only once. Putting \"a\" again replaces its value."
